    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d81">

                <pb facs="0245" n="117" xml:id="pg117"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 81.</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr.
                            Hall</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1885-04-23"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 23,
                        1885</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 241.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 329 of the 26th
                    ultimo in regard to the proposed Central American Union, and my telegram to you
                    of the 24th of that month relative to the ninth article of the decree of March
                    5, 1885, as it concerned the treaties of the several states of Central America
                    with other nations.</p>
                <p>The annulment of the decree of February 28th last (March 5), since reported, does
                    away with the importance of the incident. This Government’s inquiry was prompted
                    by the sweeping nature of the ninth article of the decree and by the uncertainty
                    as to whether it embraced all treaties which might not have been perfected by
                    ratification and exchange before February 28, the verb <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >concluir</hi> in this sense being ambiguous. Mr. Cruz’s declaration that it
                    only intended to refer to treaties <hi rendition="#italic">signed</hi> after
                    that date agrees with Mr. Batres’ announcements, but Mr. Batres did <hi
                        rendition="#italic">not</hi> add that it only intended to refer to those
                    states which adhered to the movement for union. In this aspect the logic of this
                    limitation is not evident.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c,</p>
                <closer><signed>T. F. BAYARD.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
